commit: f8978bb1
learnings:
  - INHERITED NUMBERS ARE CLAIMS, NOT FACTS. Of seven load-bearing beliefs carried into this session, seven were wrong. Not one survived contact with a measurement. The cost of checking was minutes each; the cost of acting on any one of them would have been a fix aimed at the wrong mechanism, or a false alarm to the founder.
  - TWO OF THE WRONG BELIEFS WERE MANUFACTURED IN-SESSION, WHICH MEANS FRESHNESS IS NOT ACCURACY. I reported '55.9% junk' without naming which of the three absorb surfaces it came from, producing a false mass-retirement alarm; and I shipped a rename fix keyed on stableKey (an opaque hash) that matched zero pairs and changed nothing. A correct-looking no-op is the hardest defect to see, because success and failure render identically.
  - 'THE SAME DEFECT SHAPE APPEARED FIVE TIMES IN ONE DAY ACROSS UNRELATED SUBSYSTEMS: health reading one of two writers; my un-surfaced measurement; the worktree leaf-name collision; the orchestrator dropping 18 of 26 agents; my no-op rename fix. In every case two records diverged and the failure was byte-identical to success. This is the dominant failure mode of this codebase and deserves a standing audit question: for every counter, enumerate ALL writers; for every fix, prove the output CHANGED.'
  - A FALSIFIER FINDS WHAT ITS CRITERIA DO NOT ASK FOR. The 40-seal scale run passed all six pre-registered criteria and then surfaced the rename/grade corruption, which no criterion covered. Pre-registration protects against moving the goalposts; it does not bound what the exercise can teach. Run the thing even when you expect it to pass.
  - PRODUCT CLAIMS DECAY INTO FICTION WHERE THEY ARE UNTESTED. 'Renames are the EMPTY delta' was in the CLI help and the module header, true for .purpose symbols and structurally impossible for code-units since the code-lens shipped. A documented invariant with no test is a belief, and it silently cost 29% of strands their calibration.
  - 'DRIVE THE SYSTEM TO PRODUCE ONE OF THE THING BEFORE TRUSTING A ZERO. ''This project has never produced a KNOT'' was partly the instrument: health counted only shadow rows while the native path writes payloads to .warpline/knots/. One hand-driven KNOT on a scratch repo falsified the belief in under an hour.'
  - EXONERATE-OR-CONVICT BY EXPERIMENT. When the suite began failing after my changes, the plausible story was that my new subprocess-spawning tests caused it. Removing them and re-running showed the suite fail WORSE (3 failures vs 1), which is what pointed at the real git worktree TOCTOU race. Plausibility would have had me delete good tests and ship the bug.
